{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.53538708ae","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-326","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Cresthill Bridge is in central Canada. It opened in 1983.</li><li>Its main span is 1,001 meters.</li><li>The Sable Bridge is in southern Norway. It opened in 1975.</li><li>Its main span is 987 meters.</li><li>Both are suspension bridges.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two bridges opened.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Both the Cresthill Bridge and the Sable Bridge are suspension bridges.","The Cresthill Bridge is in central Canada, and the Sable Bridge is in southern Norway.","The Cresthill Bridge opened in 1983, while the Sable Bridge opened in 1975.","The main span of the Cresthill Bridge (1,001 meters) is greater than that of the Sable Bridge (987 meters)."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.186fc13053","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-329","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Lake Sable is in southern Brazil. It was first surveyed in 1977.</li><li>Its surface area is 384 square kilometers.</li><li>Lake Eastwick is in northern Chile. It was first surveyed in 1925.</li><li>Its surface area is 205 square kilometers.</li><li>Both are reservoirs created by dams.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize how the surface area of the two lakes differs.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Both Lake Sable and Lake Eastwick are reservoirs created by dams.","Lake Sable, which is in southern Brazil, is one of several reservoirs created by dams.","Lake Sable was first surveyed in 1977, while Lake Eastwick was first surveyed in 1925.","The surface area of Lake Sable (384 square kilometers) is greater than that of Lake Eastwick (205 square kilometers)."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It compares the surface area of both lakes. The other choices describe a similarity, a difference in date, or only one lake."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.2c3dfb256c","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-330","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Juniper Tunnel is in northern India. It opened in 1908.</li><li>Its length is 53 kilometers.</li><li>The Wexford Tunnel is in southern Brazil. It opened in 1966.</li><li>Its length is 39 kilometers.</li><li>Both are tunnels that pass under a mountain range.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two tunnels opened.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Juniper Tunnel is in northern India, and the Wexford Tunnel is in southern Brazil.","The length of the Juniper Tunnel (53 kilometers) is greater than that of the Wexford Tunnel (39 kilometers).","The Juniper Tunnel opened in 1908, while the Wexford Tunnel opened in 1966.","Both the Juniper Tunnel and the Wexford Tunnel are tunnels that pass under a mountain range."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.cd237da640","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-333","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Mount Valewood is in northern Japan. It was first climbed in 1895.</li><li>Its height is 4,187 meters.</li><li>Mount Glenrock is in northern Chile. It was first climbed in 1987.</li><li>Its height is 3,886 meters.</li><li>Both are peaks in the same mountain range.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize how the height of the two mountains differs.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Mount Valewood, which is in northern Japan, is one of several peaks in the same mountain range.","Mount Valewood was first climbed in 1895, while Mount Glenrock was first climbed in 1987.","The height of Mount Valewood (4,187 meters) is greater than that of Mount Glenrock (3,886 meters).","Both Mount Valewood and Mount Glenrock are peaks in the same mountain range."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It compares the height of both mountains. The other choices describe a similarity, a difference in date, or only one mountain."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.062e95209d","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-339","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Larkspur Tower is in central Canada. It was completed in 1954.</li><li>Its height is 395 meters.</li><li>The Cresthill Tower is in central Spain. It was completed in 1969.</li><li>Its height is 115 meters.</li><li>Both are towers built mainly of steel.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two towers were completed.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Both the Larkspur Tower and the Cresthill Tower are towers built mainly of steel.","The height of the Larkspur Tower (395 meters) is greater than that of the Cresthill Tower (115 meters).","The Larkspur Tower is in central Canada, and the Cresthill Tower is in central Spain.","The Larkspur Tower was completed in 1954, while the Cresthill Tower was completed in 1969."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.4d007da5f3","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-345","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Fenwick Observatory is in western Kenya. It began operating in 1925.</li><li>Its main telescope's mirror diameter is 10 meters.</li><li>The Cresthill Observatory is in southern New Zealand. It began operating in 1912.</li><li>Its main telescope's mirror diameter is 6 meters.</li><li>Both are observatories located on remote mountaintops.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast the locations of the two observatorys.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Fenwick Observatory is in western Kenya, and the Cresthill Observatory is in southern New Zealand.","The main telescope's mirror diameter of the Fenwick Observatory (10 meters) is greater than that of the Cresthill Observatory (6 meters).","The Fenwick Observatory began operating in 1925, while the Cresthill Observatory began operating in 1912.","The Fenwick Observatory, which is in western Kenya, is one of several observatories located on remote mountaintops."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It names where both are and contrasts them. The other choices compare dates or size, or mention only one location."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.2ce0494603","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-353","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Lake Orla is in western Scotland. It was first surveyed in 1972.</li><li>Its surface area is 731 square kilometers.</li><li>Lake Wexford is in northern India. It was first surveyed in 2012.</li><li>Its surface area is 620 square kilometers.</li><li>Both are freshwater lakes formed by glaciers.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast the locations of the two lakes.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The surface area of Lake Orla (731 square kilometers) is greater than that of Lake Wexford (620 square kilometers).","Lake Orla is in western Scotland, and Lake Wexford is in northern India.","Lake Orla, which is in western Scotland, is one of several freshwater lakes formed by glaciers.","Lake Orla was first surveyed in 1972, while Lake Wexford was first surveyed in 2012."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It names where both are and contrasts them. The other choices compare dates or size, or mention only one location."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.7625297f33","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-356","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Yarrow Museum is in central Spain. It opened in 1951.</li><li>Its annual attendance is 896,590 visitors.</li><li>The Brookhaven Museum is in northern Chile. It opened in 1936.</li><li>Its annual attendance is 293,186 visitors.</li><li>Both are museums of natural history.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast the locations of the two museums.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Yarrow Museum is in central Spain, and the Brookhaven Museum is in northern Chile.","The Yarrow Museum opened in 1951, while the Brookhaven Museum opened in 1936.","The annual attendance of the Yarrow Museum (896,590 visitors) is greater than that of the Brookhaven Museum (293,186 visitors).","The Yarrow Museum, which is in central Spain, is one of several museums of natural history."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It names where both are and contrasts them. The other choices compare dates or size, or mention only one location."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.c3fc0afe36","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-373","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Lake Cresthill is in southern Norway. It was first surveyed in 1932.</li><li>Its surface area is 560 square kilometers.</li><li>Lake Larkspur is in southern New Zealand. It was first surveyed in 1976.</li><li>Its surface area is 389 square kilometers.</li><li>Both are freshwater lakes formed by glaciers.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two lakes were first surveyed.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Lake Cresthill was first surveyed in 1932, while Lake Larkspur was first surveyed in 1976.","Both Lake Cresthill and Lake Larkspur are freshwater lakes formed by glaciers.","Lake Cresthill is in southern Norway, and Lake Larkspur is in southern New Zealand.","The surface area of Lake Cresthill (560 square kilometers) is greater than that of Lake Larkspur (389 square kilometers)."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.2743b9560d","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-378","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Mount Ironwood is in central Spain. It was first climbed in 1956.</li><li>Its height is 6,389 meters.</li><li>Mount Glenrock is in eastern Poland. It was first climbed in 1935.</li><li>Its height is 2,655 meters.</li><li>Both are peaks in the same mountain range.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between Mount Ironwood and Mount Glenrock.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Mount Ironwood was first climbed in 1956, while Mount Glenrock was first climbed in 1935.","Both Mount Ironwood and Mount Glenrock are peaks in the same mountain range.","Mount Ironwood is in central Spain, and Mount Glenrock is in eastern Poland.","The height of Mount Ironwood (6,389 meters) is greater than that of Mount Glenrock (2,655 meters)."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. Only this choice describes something the two share. The others present differences (in size, date, or location)."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.8fab8cf966","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-380","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Orla Observatory is in northern India. It began operating in 1966.</li><li>Its main telescope's mirror diameter is 10 meters.</li><li>The Cresthill Observatory is in eastern Australia. It began operating in 2010.</li><li>Its main telescope's mirror diameter is 7 meters.</li><li>Both are radio observatories.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between the Orla Observatory and the Cresthill Observatory.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Orla Observatory began operating in 1966, while the Cresthill Observatory began operating in 2010.","Both the Orla Observatory and the Cresthill Observatory are radio observatories.","The Orla Observatory is in northern India, and the Cresthill Observatory is in eastern Australia.","The main telescope's mirror diameter of the Orla Observatory (10 meters) is greater than that of the Cresthill Observatory (7 meters)."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. Only this choice describes something the two share. The others present differences (in size, date, or location)."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.55cac439b6","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-392","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Glenrock Tower is in western Kenya. It was completed in 2018.</li><li>Its height is 488 meters.</li><li>The Quarry Tower is in eastern Poland. It was completed in 1922.</li><li>Its height is 101 meters.</li><li>Both are towers built mainly of steel.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast the locations of the two towers.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Glenrock Tower is in western Kenya, and the Quarry Tower is in eastern Poland.","The Glenrock Tower was completed in 2018, while the Quarry Tower was completed in 1922.","The Glenrock Tower, which is in western Kenya, is one of several towers built mainly of steel.","The height of the Glenrock Tower (488 meters) is greater than that of the Quarry Tower (101 meters)."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It names where both are and contrasts them. The other choices compare dates or size, or mention only one location."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.6935caf249","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-1","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Omar Brennan is a marine biologist.</li><li>Brennan wanted to find out whether noise from passing ships affects how often humpback whales sing.</li><li>Brennan recorded whale songs in a busy shipping lane and in a quiet bay for two months.</li><li>Brennan found that whales in the shipping lane sang about 40 percent less often.</li><li>Brennan concluded that ship noise may interfere with whale communication.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Brennan concluded that ship noise may interfere with whale communication.","Marine biologist Omar Brennan set out to learn whether noise from passing ships affects how often humpback whales sing.","To investigate the question, Brennan recorded whale songs in a busy shipping lane and in a quiet bay for two months.","Brennan found that whales in the shipping lane sang about 40 percent less often."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.e8654c0222","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-6","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Sofia Halvorsen is an ecologist.</li><li>Halvorsen wanted to find out whether city lights change when songbirds begin singing in the morning.</li><li>Halvorsen compared the first songs of robins in brightly lit city parks with those in dark rural woodlands.</li><li>Halvorsen found that robins in lit parks began singing nearly an hour earlier.</li><li>Halvorsen concluded that artificial light may shift birds' daily rhythms.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Halvorsen compared the first songs of robins in brightly lit city parks with those in dark rural woodlands.","Ecologist Sofia Halvorsen set out to learn whether city lights change when songbirds begin singing in the morning.","Halvorsen found that robins in lit parks began singing nearly an hour earlier.","Halvorsen concluded that artificial light may shift birds' daily rhythms."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.847b2e5737","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-6","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Sofia Halvorsen is an ecologist.</li><li>Halvorsen wanted to find out whether city lights change when songbirds begin singing in the morning.</li><li>Halvorsen compared the first songs of robins in brightly lit city parks with those in dark rural woodlands.</li><li>Halvorsen found that robins in lit parks began singing nearly an hour earlier.</li><li>Halvorsen concluded that artificial light may shift birds' daily rhythms.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Halvorsen compared the first songs of robins in brightly lit city parks with those in dark rural woodlands.","Because robins in lit parks began singing nearly an hour earlier, Halvorsen concluded that artificial light may shift birds' daily rhythms.","Halvorsen concluded that artificial light may shift birds' daily rhythms.","Halvorsen found that robins in lit parks began singing nearly an hour earlier."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.5e2085b0a1","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-17","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Zara Nakamura is a psychologist.</li><li>Nakamura wanted to find out whether taking short breaks improves students' focus during long study sessions.</li><li>Nakamura had one group of students take a five-minute break every half hour while another group studied without breaks.</li><li>Nakamura found that students who took breaks answered more practice questions correctly.</li><li>Nakamura concluded that brief breaks may help people stay focused.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Psychologist Zara Nakamura set out to learn whether taking short breaks improves students' focus during long study sessions.","Nakamura found that students who took breaks answered more practice questions correctly.","To investigate the question, Nakamura had one group of students take a five-minute break every half hour while another group studied without breaks.","Nakamura concluded that brief breaks may help people stay focused."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.8084005ba6","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-26","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Kofi Moreau is a materials scientist.</li><li>Moreau wanted to find out whether a coating made from seaweed can keep fruit fresh longer.</li><li>Moreau coated half of a batch of strawberries with a seaweed-based film and left the rest uncoated.</li><li>Moreau found that coated strawberries stayed fresh for about four days longer.</li><li>Moreau concluded that the coating could help reduce food waste.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Moreau coated half of a batch of strawberries with a seaweed-based film and left the rest uncoated.","Moreau concluded that the coating could help reduce food waste.","Moreau found that coated strawberries stayed fresh for about four days longer.","Materials scientist Kofi Moreau set out to learn whether a coating made from seaweed can keep fruit fresh longer."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.18bafb49e4","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-28","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Ayumi Okafor is a materials scientist.</li><li>Okafor wanted to find out whether a coating made from seaweed can keep fruit fresh longer.</li><li>Okafor coated half of a batch of strawberries with a seaweed-based film and left the rest uncoated.</li><li>Okafor found that coated strawberries stayed fresh for about four days longer.</li><li>Okafor concluded that the coating could help reduce food waste.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Okafor concluded that the coating could help reduce food waste.","Materials scientist Ayumi Okafor set out to learn whether a coating made from seaweed can keep fruit fresh longer.","Okafor found that coated strawberries stayed fresh for about four days longer.","To investigate the question, Okafor coated half of a batch of strawberries with a seaweed-based film and left the rest uncoated."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.1fec25d69e","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-32","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Omar Asante is an entomologist.</li><li>Asante wanted to find out whether bees prefer flowers of a particular color.</li><li>Asante set out identical feeders colored blue, yellow, and white in a meadow.</li><li>Asante found that bees visited the blue feeders most often.</li><li>Asante concluded that color may play a role in how bees choose flowers.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Asante set out identical feeders colored blue, yellow, and white in a meadow.","Asante found that bees visited the blue feeders most often.","Entomologist Omar Asante set out to learn whether bees prefer flowers of a particular color.","Asante concluded that color may play a role in how bees choose flowers."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.febce7fd4c","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-51","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Priya Abara is a nutrition researcher.</li><li>Abara wanted to find out whether eating breakfast affects how much food people eat at lunch.</li><li>Abara asked volunteers to eat breakfast on some days and skip it on others, then measured their lunch portions.</li><li>Abara found that lunch portions were about the same on both kinds of days.</li><li>Abara concluded that skipping breakfast may not lead people to eat more later.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Abara found that lunch portions were about the same on both kinds of days.","Nutrition researcher Priya Abara set out to learn whether eating breakfast affects how much food people eat at lunch.","To investigate the question, Abara asked volunteers to eat breakfast on some days and skip it on others, then measured their lunch portions.","Abara concluded that skipping breakfast may not lead people to eat more later."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.dbbc962652","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-53","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Felipe Kowalski is a nutrition researcher.</li><li>Kowalski wanted to find out whether eating breakfast affects how much food people eat at lunch.</li><li>Kowalski asked volunteers to eat breakfast on some days and skip it on others, then measured their lunch portions.</li><li>Kowalski found that lunch portions were about the same on both kinds of days.</li><li>Kowalski concluded that skipping breakfast may not lead people to eat more later.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Kowalski concluded that skipping breakfast may not lead people to eat more later.","To investigate the question, Kowalski asked volunteers to eat breakfast on some days and skip it on others, then measured their lunch portions.","Nutrition researcher Felipe Kowalski set out to learn whether eating breakfast affects how much food people eat at lunch.","Kowalski found that lunch portions were about the same on both kinds of days."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.241be127eb","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-56","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Beatriz Haddad is a zoologist.</li><li>Haddad wanted to find out whether zoo elephants that are given puzzle feeders are more active.</li><li>Haddad gave puzzle feeders to some elephants and standard food trays to others, then tracked their movement.</li><li>Haddad found that elephants with puzzle feeders walked noticeably more each day.</li><li>Haddad concluded that puzzle feeders may encourage healthy activity.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Haddad concluded that puzzle feeders may encourage healthy activity.","Haddad found that elephants with puzzle feeders walked noticeably more each day.","Because elephants with puzzle feeders walked noticeably more each day, Haddad concluded that puzzle feeders may encourage healthy activity.","To investigate the question, Haddad gave puzzle feeders to some elephants and standard food trays to others, then tracked their movement."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.41e5a5c212","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-59","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Ingrid Haddad is a zoologist.</li><li>Haddad wanted to find out whether zoo elephants that are given puzzle feeders are more active.</li><li>Haddad gave puzzle feeders to some elephants and standard food trays to others, then tracked their movement.</li><li>Haddad found that elephants with puzzle feeders walked noticeably more each day.</li><li>Haddad concluded that puzzle feeders may encourage healthy activity.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Haddad found that elephants with puzzle feeders walked noticeably more each day.","To investigate the question, Haddad gave puzzle feeders to some elephants and standard food trays to others, then tracked their movement.","Zoologist Ingrid Haddad set out to learn whether zoo elephants that are given puzzle feeders are more active.","Haddad concluded that puzzle feeders may encourage healthy activity."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.88dd49eb6c","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-68","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Tomás Moreau is a linguist.</li><li>Moreau wanted to find out whether bilingual children are better at switching between tasks.</li><li>Moreau gave bilingual and monolingual children a game that required switching rules quickly.</li><li>Moreau found that bilingual children switched rules with fewer errors.</li><li>Moreau concluded that speaking two languages may strengthen certain mental skills.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Moreau found that bilingual children switched rules with fewer errors.","Linguist Tomás Moreau set out to learn whether bilingual children are better at switching between tasks.","Moreau concluded that speaking two languages may strengthen certain mental skills.","To investigate the question, Moreau gave bilingual and monolingual children a game that required switching rules quickly."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.229f362714","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-78","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Ayumi Abara is a botanist.</li><li>Abara wanted to find out whether tomato plants grow better with music played nearby.</li><li>Abara grew tomato plants in two identical greenhouses, one with music and one without.</li><li>Abara found that plants in both greenhouses grew at similar rates.</li><li>Abara concluded that music may have little effect on tomato growth.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Abara concluded that music may have little effect on tomato growth.","Botanist Ayumi Abara set out to learn whether tomato plants grow better with music played nearby.","Abara found that plants in both greenhouses grew at similar rates.","To investigate the question, Abara grew tomato plants in two identical greenhouses, one with music and one without."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.f748c13b0d","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-83","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Zara Nakamura is a botanist.</li><li>Nakamura wanted to find out whether tomato plants grow better with music played nearby.</li><li>Nakamura grew tomato plants in two identical greenhouses, one with music and one without.</li><li>Nakamura found that plants in both greenhouses grew at similar rates.</li><li>Nakamura concluded that music may have little effect on tomato growth.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Because plants in both greenhouses grew at similar rates, Nakamura concluded that music may have little effect on tomato growth.","Nakamura found that plants in both greenhouses grew at similar rates.","Nakamura concluded that music may have little effect on tomato growth.","To investigate the question, Nakamura grew tomato plants in two identical greenhouses, one with music and one without."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.4d348dc9ec","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-97","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Sofia Quintero is an education researcher.</li><li>Quintero wanted to find out whether reading aloud to young children improves their vocabulary.</li><li>Quintero had parents in one group read aloud daily while another group continued their usual routines.</li><li>Quintero found that children in the read-aloud group learned more new words.</li><li>Quintero concluded that regular read-aloud time may support vocabulary growth.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Quintero found that children in the read-aloud group learned more new words.","To investigate the question, Quintero had parents in one group read aloud daily while another group continued their usual routines.","Quintero concluded that regular read-aloud time may support vocabulary growth.","Education researcher Sofia Quintero set out to learn whether reading aloud to young children improves their vocabulary."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.f010ab0de0","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-106","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Yara Brennan is a chemist.</li><li>Brennan wanted to find out whether a new plant-based plastic breaks down in seawater.</li><li>Brennan placed samples of the plastic in tanks of seawater for six months.</li><li>Brennan found that most samples broke apart within four months.</li><li>Brennan concluded that the plastic may be less harmful to oceans than conventional plastic.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Brennan concluded that the plastic may be less harmful to oceans than conventional plastic.","Brennan found that most samples broke apart within four months.","To investigate the question, Brennan placed samples of the plastic in tanks of seawater for six months.","Chemist Yara Brennan set out to learn whether a new plant-based plastic breaks down in seawater."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.936dce9c56","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-108","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Leila Varga is a primatologist.</li><li>Varga wanted to find out whether young chimpanzees learn to use tools by watching adults.</li><li>Varga observed young chimpanzees in groups where adults cracked nuts with stones and in groups where adults did not.</li><li>Varga found that young chimpanzees in the nut-cracking groups began using stones much earlier.</li><li>Varga concluded that young chimpanzees may learn tool use by observation.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Varga found that young chimpanzees in the nut-cracking groups began using stones much earlier.","Primatologist Leila Varga set out to learn whether young chimpanzees learn to use tools by watching adults.","To investigate the question, Varga observed young chimpanzees in groups where adults cracked nuts with stones and in groups where adults did not.","Varga concluded that young chimpanzees may learn tool use by observation."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.e2762b851f","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-109","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Lucía Petrov is a primatologist.</li><li>Petrov wanted to find out whether young chimpanzees learn to use tools by watching adults.</li><li>Petrov observed young chimpanzees in groups where adults cracked nuts with stones and in groups where adults did not.</li><li>Petrov found that young chimpanzees in the nut-cracking groups began using stones much earlier.</li><li>Petrov concluded that young chimpanzees may learn tool use by observation.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Petrov observed young chimpanzees in groups where adults cracked nuts with stones and in groups where adults did not.","Petrov found that young chimpanzees in the nut-cracking groups began using stones much earlier.","Primatologist Lucía Petrov set out to learn whether young chimpanzees learn to use tools by watching adults.","Petrov concluded that young chimpanzees may learn tool use by observation."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.0a2203ab18","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-110","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Priya Mensah is a primatologist.</li><li>Mensah wanted to find out whether young chimpanzees learn to use tools by watching adults.</li><li>Mensah observed young chimpanzees in groups where adults cracked nuts with stones and in groups where adults did not.</li><li>Mensah found that young chimpanzees in the nut-cracking groups began using stones much earlier.</li><li>Mensah concluded that young chimpanzees may learn tool use by observation.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Mensah found that young chimpanzees in the nut-cracking groups began using stones much earlier.","Mensah concluded that young chimpanzees may learn tool use by observation.","To investigate the question, Mensah observed young chimpanzees in groups where adults cracked nuts with stones and in groups where adults did not.","Primatologist Priya Mensah set out to learn whether young chimpanzees learn to use tools by watching adults."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.464178ddde","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-113","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Ravi Delacroix is a primatologist.</li><li>Delacroix wanted to find out whether young chimpanzees learn to use tools by watching adults.</li><li>Delacroix observed young chimpanzees in groups where adults cracked nuts with stones and in groups where adults did not.</li><li>Delacroix found that young chimpanzees in the nut-cracking groups began using stones much earlier.</li><li>Delacroix concluded that young chimpanzees may learn tool use by observation.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Delacroix found that young chimpanzees in the nut-cracking groups began using stones much earlier.","Primatologist Ravi Delacroix set out to learn whether young chimpanzees learn to use tools by watching adults.","To investigate the question, Delacroix observed young chimpanzees in groups where adults cracked nuts with stones and in groups where adults did not.","Delacroix concluded that young chimpanzees may learn tool use by observation."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.17bd147a3a","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-132","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Amara Okafor is an archaeologist.</li><li>Okafor wanted to find out whether a coastal settlement traded with inland villages.</li><li>Okafor analyzed the minerals in pottery found at the settlement.</li><li>Okafor found that many pots were made from clay found only far inland.</li><li>Okafor concluded that the settlement may have traded with inland communities.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Okafor found that many pots were made from clay found only far inland.","Okafor concluded that the settlement may have traded with inland communities.","To investigate the question, Okafor analyzed the minerals in pottery found at the settlement.","Because many pots were made from clay found only far inland, Okafor concluded that the settlement may have traded with inland communities."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.ccd01e30b4","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-134","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Ravi Duarte is an archaeologist.</li><li>Duarte wanted to find out whether a coastal settlement traded with inland villages.</li><li>Duarte analyzed the minerals in pottery found at the settlement.</li><li>Duarte found that many pots were made from clay found only far inland.</li><li>Duarte concluded that the settlement may have traded with inland communities.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Duarte analyzed the minerals in pottery found at the settlement.","Archaeologist Ravi Duarte set out to learn whether a coastal settlement traded with inland villages.","Duarte concluded that the settlement may have traded with inland communities.","Duarte found that many pots were made from clay found only far inland."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.1df0ffc79e","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-151","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Chen Duarte is a climate scientist.</li><li>Duarte wanted to find out whether light-colored roofs reduce indoor temperatures.</li><li>Duarte compared indoor temperatures in homes with white roofs and homes with dark roofs.</li><li>Duarte found that homes with white roofs stayed several degrees cooler.</li><li>Duarte concluded that light-colored roofs may reduce the need for air-conditioning.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Duarte compared indoor temperatures in homes with white roofs and homes with dark roofs.","Climate scientist Chen Duarte set out to learn whether light-colored roofs reduce indoor temperatures.","Duarte found that homes with white roofs stayed several degrees cooler.","Duarte concluded that light-colored roofs may reduce the need for air-conditioning."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.3e1eef0482","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-155","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Ravi Okafor is a climate scientist.</li><li>Okafor wanted to find out whether light-colored roofs reduce indoor temperatures.</li><li>Okafor compared indoor temperatures in homes with white roofs and homes with dark roofs.</li><li>Okafor found that homes with white roofs stayed several degrees cooler.</li><li>Okafor concluded that light-colored roofs may reduce the need for air-conditioning.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Climate scientist Ravi Okafor set out to learn whether light-colored roofs reduce indoor temperatures.","To investigate the question, Okafor compared indoor temperatures in homes with white roofs and homes with dark roofs.","Okafor concluded that light-colored roofs may reduce the need for air-conditioning.","Okafor found that homes with white roofs stayed several degrees cooler."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.32593e626f","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-159","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Samuel Delacroix is a behavioral economist.</li><li>Delacroix wanted to find out whether shoppers buy more fruit when it is placed near the store entrance.</li><li>Delacroix moved the fruit display to the entrance in some stores but not others.</li><li>Delacroix found that fruit sales rose in the stores with entrance displays.</li><li>Delacroix concluded that product placement may influence shoppers' choices.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Delacroix found that fruit sales rose in the stores with entrance displays.","Delacroix concluded that product placement may influence shoppers' choices.","To investigate the question, Delacroix moved the fruit display to the entrance in some stores but not others.","Because fruit sales rose in the stores with entrance displays, Delacroix concluded that product placement may influence shoppers' choices."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.d5adc4f35d","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-162","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Kenji Abara is a veterinary scientist.</li><li>Abara wanted to find out whether dogs recognize their owners' voices.</li><li>Abara played recordings of owners' voices and strangers' voices to dogs.</li><li>Abara found that dogs turned toward their owners' voices far more often.</li><li>Abara concluded that dogs may be able to tell their owners' voices apart from others.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Abara found that dogs turned toward their owners' voices far more often.","To investigate the question, Abara played recordings of owners' voices and strangers' voices to dogs.","Veterinary scientist Kenji Abara set out to learn whether dogs recognize their owners' voices.","Abara concluded that dogs may be able to tell their owners' voices apart from others."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."}
